# DESCRIPTION

kbdfs turns the keys that keyboard drivers push to it into the text a program reads, as 9front's kbdfs does.
A manifest mounts its tree at `/dev`, before whatever else is there.
Its own manifest mounts the console at `/mnt/console` and proc(4) at `/proc`, for the echo and the interrupt.
A keyboard driver (virtio-input(3) is one) writes each key it sees to `kbin`; kbdfs makes a rune of it by the keymap and the modifiers held, and gives the rune to the program reading `cons`, or to the one reading `kbd`.
A read of `kbd` or `cons` waits until there is something to return.

## Keys

A key is a HID usage of the keyboard page.
Its rune comes from the keymap's layer for the modifiers held: `altgrmod4` for AltGr with the Mod4 key, `mod4`, `shiftaltgr`, `shift`, `altgr`, `ctl`, or else `none`; the first three apply only to keys that have a rune in that layer.
The keymap is a US keyboard.
As in 9front's, Enter is a newline, Backspace is `^H`, Delete is the key Kdel, and CapsLock is Ctl.
The keypad moves the cursor (Home, End, the arrows and the others) until NumLock is pressed, then types digits.
The keys that are not characters, such as the arrows and the function keys, are runes of Unicode's private use area, as in Plan 9's keyboard.h.

## cons

A read of `cons` returns what was typed, in UTF-8, and waits if nothing has been.
Typing is cooked unless raw mode is on.

Cooked, kbdfs holds the line being typed and echoes it to the console.
`^H` erases the last character, `^W` the last word and `^U` the whole line; the keys erase on the console with a backspace each.
The line is given to a read when a newline is typed, or at 256 characters.
`^D` gives the line so far, without a newline; typed on an empty line it gives an end of input, which a read returns as nothing, once.
Delete throws away the line being typed and posts an `interrupt` note to kbdfs's note group, through proc(4)'s `notepg`: the console's shell, `rc`, is in it, with what it runs (see manifest(6)'s `notegroup`).
kbdfs takes its own notes and ignores the interrupt.
A read that asks for less than the line gets it in parts.

Raw, each rune is given to a read as it is typed, with no echo and no editing; Delete is a rune like the others.

A write to `cons` is shown on the console, as the echo is: kbdfs writes both to the console's `write`, at `/mnt/console` in its namespace, or to the system log while there is none.
While `kbd` is open, `cons` hears nothing: the runes go to `kbd`.

## kbd

A read of `kbd` returns messages, each ending in a NUL byte, as many whole ones as fit the read.
`kbd` has its messages only while it is open.

: `k<keys>`
  A key went down.
  <keys> are the keys held, in the order pressed, each as the rune of the key without the modifiers, so Shift and `a` held is the Shift rune and `a`.
: `K<keys>`
  A key was released; <keys> are those still held.
: `c<rune>`
  The rune typed.

## consctl

A write of `rawon` to `consctl` turns raw mode on, and `rawoff` turns it off.
A write that begins with neither is refused as invalid.
Raw mode lasts only as long as a channel is open on `consctl`; when the last closes, the console is cooked again.
Turning raw mode on gives the line typed so far to `cons` at once.
`consctl` reads as empty.

## kbmap

A read of `kbmap` returns the keymap, a line for each rune of each layer, `layer usage rune`, the three fields right aligned to 11 characters.
The layers are `none`, `shift`, `altgr`, `ctl`, `shiftaltgr`, `mod4` and `altgrmod4`.
A write sets runes, a line each in the same form.
The layer may be a name or a number; the usage is a number below 256; the rune is a number (decimal, `0x` hexadecimal, a leading `0` octal), `'` and a character, or `^` and a letter for a control character.
Blank lines and lines starting `#` are skipped.
The first line that is not understood stops the write with an error, the lines before it kept.

## kbin and kbdin

`kbin` is for keyboard drivers.
A write is any number of two byte records: a usage, then 1 for pressed or 0 for released.
A keyboard driver gets its own channel to `kbin` from devmgr(8), at `/dev/kbin` in the driver's namespace.
All the keyboards share the modifiers held.

`kbdin` is for a program that types for someone else.
A write is messages ending in NUL: `r<rune>` presses and `R<rune>` releases the key that types the rune; `c<rune>` types it; any other message, such as `k<keys>`, goes to the readers of `kbd` as it is, if it is open.

# FILES

: `/dev/kbd`
  The keys held and typed, as messages.
: `/dev/cons`
  What was typed, as lines or runes; writes go to the console.
: `/dev/consctl`
  `rawon` and `rawoff`.
: `/dev/kbmap`
  The keymap, read and written as text.
: `/dev/kbin`
  Usages from keyboard drivers.
: `/dev/kbdin`
  Messages typed for someone else.

